# Why do we put off what we planned to do?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What is present bias? Why can you be rational today and regret it tomorrow?"
next: {"title":"Should the government nudge us?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/89-should-the-government-nudge-us.cast"}

## Paying not to go
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - 10-visit pass
      - Monthly fee
    values:
      - 10
      - 17
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      prefix: $
      decimals: 0
    icons:
      - ticket
      - infinity
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 25
    y_label: Price per visit
    x_label: Three US gyms
    gap: 0.6
    label_size: 24
    bar_colors: same
sources:
    - id: dvm
      title: Paying Not to Go to the Gym
      authors: DellaVigna, S. & Malmendier, U.
      year: 2006
      finding: '7,752 members of three US health clubs over three years. Members on a flat monthly fee of over $70 attended on average 4.3 times a month: over $17 per expected visit, against $10 a visit with a 10-visit pass. They forgo about $600 each over the membership. Leading explanation: overconfidence about future self-control or efficiency.'
      url: https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.96.3.694

    text clubs "7,752 members, 3 years" at.percent [55,90] cites ["dvm"] font_size 28 hidden true
    text visits "4.3 visits a month" above bar_2 gap 30 #b5482e cites ["dvm"] font_size 30 hidden true
    text lost "≈ $600 lost each" above bar_2 gap 30 #b5482e cites ["dvm"] font_size 32 hidden true
    text plans "Plans: go" #2f6b8f at.percent [35,90] font_size 34 hidden true
    text does "Does: skip" #b5482e at.percent [68,90] font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Paying not to go"

Why do we put off what we planned to do? Start with people who paid good money to make themselves do it.
    draw axes

B: Three American health clubs, nearly eight tho (@draw clubs@)usand members, three years of check-ins. Stefano DellaVigna and Ulrike Malmendier went through them all.

Members could (@draw bar_1@) buy a ten-visit pass, at ten dollars a visit. Or pay over seventy dollars a month and come as often as they liked.

    erase clubs

    ask "Drag the monthly members' bar to your guess of the price per visit." on bar_2 store g default "8" voice b
        intro: Your guess: for the monthly members, what did each visit really cost?
        right: Close: you said {g}. It was over seventeen dollars a visit.
        wrong: You said {g}. It was over seventeen dollars a visit.

Over seventeen dollars. They c (@draw visits@)ame, on average, just over four times a month.

    erase visits

B: So the pass would have been cheaper. The authors put the mone (@draw lost@)y left on the table at about six hundred dollars per member.

Maybe they're just lazy?
    highlight bar_2

    erase lost

B: Lazy people don't sign up for unlimited. You p (@draw plans@)ay a flat fee because you expect to go a lot.

So when they signed, they meant it. And then, w (@draw does@)eek after week, they didn't go.

B: One person, two verdicts: the one who plans and the one who has to get up and go. To see why they disagree, we need a way to weigh now against later.
    highlight plans does

## A cliff after today
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 13
    'y':
      - 0
      - 1.25
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 170
      w: 680
      h: 480
vars: {"b":{"value":1,"min":0.3,"max":1}}
sources:
    - id: pp
      title: On Second-Best National Saving and Game-Equilibrium Growth
      authors: Phelps, E. S. & Pollak, R. A.
      year: 1968
      finding: 'First statement of quasi-hyperbolic (beta-delta) discounting: the present weighted fully, every later period shrunk by an extra factor.'
    - id: laibson
      title: Golden Eggs and Hyperbolic Discounting
      authors: Laibson, D.
      year: 1997
      finding: Quasi-hyperbolic (beta-delta) discounting makes preferences dynamically inconsistent and gives consumers a motive to constrain their own future choices.
      url: https://doi.org/10.1162/003355397555253

    axes ax x_label "Weeks ahead" y_label Weight ticks.x [0,1,2,4,6,8,9] ticks.y [0.5,0.7,1] hidden true
    curve steady dashed #8f887c expr "1" x_from 0 x_to 10 hidden true
    label lab_steady "Steady" above #8f887c attach_to steady hidden true
    dot today #b5482e at.x 0 at.y 1 hidden true
    label lab_today "Today" above-right #b5482e attach_to today hidden true
    scratch polls work ["Today or 1 week: take $100","8 or 9 weeks: wait for $110"] x 540 y 300 font_size 26 hidden true
    curve fut #2f6b8f expr b x_from 1 x_to 10 hidden true
    label lab_beta below #2f6b8f attach_to fut tex "\\beta = {b:2}" hidden true
    arrow cliff #b5482e from.x 1 from.y 1 to.x 1 to.y 0.7 bind {"to.y":"b"} hidden true
    dot m8 #2f6b8f at.on fut at.x 8 hidden true
    label lab_m8 "$100 × 0.7" below #2f6b8f attach_to m8 hidden true
    dot m9 #2f6b8f at.on fut at.x 9 hidden true
    label lab_m9 "$110 × 0.7" above #2f6b8f attach_to m9 hidden true
    dot n1 #2f6b8f at.on fut at.x 1 hidden true
    label lab_n0 "$100 × 1" below-right #b5482e attach_to today hidden true
    label lab_n1 "$110 × 0.7" below-right #2f6b8f attach_to n1 hidden true
    scratch calc work ["8 vs 9 weeks: 70 < 77","Today vs 1 week: 100 > 77"] x 540 y 300 font_size 26 hidden true
    portrait laib cites ["laibson"] x 870 y 470 width 190 of "David Laibson" cameo true hidden true
    text name "β–δ discounting" top-right #2f6b8f cites ["pp","laibson"] font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "A cliff after today"

B: Planning one thing and doing another isn't ordinary impatience. A steady discount, the kind that makes savers ask for interest, never changes its mind.
    draw ax

Why not? If I'm impatient, I'm impatient.
    draw today lab_today

B: Steady impatience shrinks each week by the same small amount. Over a few weeks it's tiny, so draw it flat: every week counts the same.
    draw steady lab_steady

    quiz "$100 today, or $110 in a week?" judge false
        intro: Then let's test it on ourselves. A hundred dollars today, or a hundred and ten in a week?
        right: Noted. Now push both a couple of months out.
        * $100 today
        * $110 in a week

    quiz "$100 in 8 weeks, or $110 in 9 weeks?" judge false voice b
        intro: And a hundred in eight weeks, or a hundred and ten in nine?
        right: Same gap, same extra ten dollars. Did your answer change?
        * $100 in 8 weeks
        * $110 in 9 weeks

In surveys, plenty of people grab the hundred now, yet happily wait the extra week when both are two months away. Richard Thaler found the same steepness near now in 1981.
    draw polls

    erase polls

B: That's present bias. Today counts in full; everything lat (@animate b 0.7 duration 2.5@)er is shrunk by one extra factor, beta. Say seven tenths.
    draw fut lab_beta

So there's a cliff between now and every later week. Past it, th (@highlight fut@)e line is flat again.
    draw cliff

B: Two months out, both offers sit beyond the cliff, shrunk alike, so a hundre (@draw calc_box calc_line_1@)d and ten still beats a hundred.
    draw m8 lab_m8 m9 lab_m9

But when week eight arrives, the hundr (@draw lab_n0 n1 lab_n1@)ed is today, and the hundred and ten falls off the cliff: seventy-seven (@draw calc_line_2@) against a hundred.
    erase m8 lab_m8 m9 lab_m9

    erase calc

B: Edmund Phelps and Robert Pollak wrote this down in 1968; Davi (@draw laib@)d Laibson made it famous in 1997. It's called beta-delta, or  (@draw name@)quasi-hyperbolic, discounting.

    erase laib

Same person, same tastes. Only the date of the choice moved, and the choice flipped.
    highlight cliff today

## Monday's Ida, Tuesday's Ida
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 4
    'y':
      - -6
      - 6
    box:
      x: 50
      'y': 150
      w: 600
      h: 510
vars:
    b:
      value: 0.7
      min: 0.3
      max: 1
    mon:
      expr: 4*b
    tue:
      expr: 14*b - 10

    axes ax y_label "Net value of Tuesday's workout" ticks.x [] ticks.y [-4,-2,0,2,4] hidden true
    curve zero #3d3833 expr "0" x_from 0 x_to 4 hidden true
    curve montop #2f6b8f expr mon x_from 0.55 x_to 1.45 hidden true
    region monbar x_from 0.55 x_to 1.45 between ["zero","montop"] style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.35 hidden true
    curve tuetop #b5482e expr tue x_from 2.35 x_to 3.25 hidden true
    region tuebar x_from 2.35 x_to 3.25 between ["zero","tuetop"] style.fill #b5482e style.opacity 0.35 hidden true
    text lab_mon "Monday plans" #2f6b8f at.data [1,-5.3] font_size 26 hidden true
    text lab_tue "Tuesday decides" #b5482e at.data [2.8,-5.3] font_size 26 hidden true
    text val_mon "{mon:1}" #2f6b8f at.data [1,4.7] font_size 28 bind {"at.data.1":"mon + 0.6"} hidden true
    text val_tue "{tue:1}" #b5482e at.data [2.8,4.7] font_size 28 bind {"at.data.1":"max(tue,0) + 0.6"} hidden true
    text go "goes" #8f887c at.data [3.75,2.5] font_size 30 hidden true
    text skip "skips" #8f887c at.data [3.75,-2.5] font_size 30 hidden true
    text k1 "Effort, Tue: −10" #b5482e x 835 y 560 font_size 28 hidden true
    text k2 "Health, Wed on: +14" #2f6b8f x 835 y 510 font_size 28 hidden true
    math beq tex "\\beta = {b}" size 34 x 835 y 330 hidden true
    scratch math work [{"tex":"U_t = u_t + \\beta\\,(\\delta u_{t+1} + \\delta^2 u_{t+2} + \\dots)"},{"tex":"\\text{Mon: } \\beta\\,(14-10) > 0 \\text{ for any } \\beta"},{"tex":"\\text{Tue: } 14\\beta - 10 > 0 \\iff \\beta > 0.71"}] x 760 y 450 font_size 22 hidden true

    card title "Monday's Ida, Tuesday's Ida"

B: That cliff is all we need for the gym. Say Ida, a clerk at Harbourtown's bank, plans a workout for Tuesday.
    draw ax zero

B: Illustrative numbers:  (@draw k1 k2@)it costs her ten in effort on the day, and pays back fourteen in health from Wednesday on.

On Monday, both the effort and the payof (@draw beq montop monbar val_mon lab_mon go@)f lie beyond the cliff. Both shrink by beta, so fourteen still beats ten, and she plans to go.

B: Now it's Tues (@draw lab_tue skip@)day morning. What does she do?

She goes, surely. Nothing has changed since Monday.
    highlight lab_tue

    quiz "Tuesday morning, β = 0.7. Does Ida go to the gym?" judge false voice b
        right: Let's see what Tuesday's Ida weighs.
        * She goes
        * She skips

B: One thing has: the effort is today now, at full weight (@draw tuetop tuebar val_tue@). Ten against fourteen times seven tenths, which is 9.8 (@highlight tuebar val_tue@). She skips.

And plans to go on Wednesday, because from Tuesday, Wednesday is beyond the cliff.
    highlight monbar effect box

B: That's time inconsistency: each day's plan is (@point at.ref tuebar gesture circle@) sensible, and each day's self overrules it.

Try it: slide beta and find where Tuesday's Ida still goes.
    explore params ["b"]

B: Above about (@animate b 0.75 duration 1.5@) seven tenths, the two Idas agree. At beta equal to one the (@animate b 1 duration 1.5@)re's no cliff, and nothing to regret.

    hide k1 k2 go skip beq

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: Want the formula behind the two bars?

@math

    hide mathq

B: Each day's self counts (@draw math_box math_line_1@) today in full, and everything after times beta, on top of ordinary discounting, delta.

B: With delta at one, Monday valu (@draw math_line_2@)es the workout at beta times fourteen minus ten: positive for any beta.

B: Tuesday values it at fourtee (@draw math_line_3@)n beta minus ten, positive only above ten fourteenths, about 0.71.

    erase math

@after

    hide mathq

    show k1 k2 go skip beq

    pause 0.3

## Ropes, doubts and a test
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Monday's you would be proud.
    good: Most of the cliff, spotted.
    poor: Plan to try again tomorrow. Really.
    none: Skipped? We'll put it down to present bias.
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 4
    'y':
      - -6
      - 6
    box:
      x: 50
      'y': 150
      w: 600
      h: 510
vars:
    b:
      value: 0.7
      fixed: true
    f: 0
    mon:
      expr: 4*b
    tue:
      expr: 14*b - 10 + f
sources:
    - id: aky
      title: 'Tying Odysseus to the Mast: Evidence from a Commitment Savings Product in the Philippines'
      authors: Ashraf, N., Karlan, D. & Yin, W.
      year: 2006
      finding: Of 710 bank clients offered a SEED account (withdrawals restricted until a goal date or amount), 202 (28.4%) opened one; savings rose for the group offered it.
      url: https://doi.org/10.1162/qjec.2006.121.2.635
    - id: dvm
      title: Paying Not to Go to the Gym
      authors: DellaVigna, S. & Malmendier, U.
      year: 2006
      finding: 'Monthly members paid over $17 per visit against $10 with a 10-visit pass; leading explanation: overconfidence about future self-control or efficiency.'
      url: https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.96.3.694
    - id: ans
      title: 'Working over Time: Dynamic Inconsistency in Real Effort Tasks'
      authors: Augenblick, N., Niederle, M. & Sprenger, C.
      year: 2015
      finding: Very limited time inconsistency in choices over money; considerably more present bias in choices over effort. Present bias in effort predicts demand for a binding commitment device.
      url: https://doi.org/10.1093/qje/qjv020

    axes ax y_label "Net value of Tuesday's workout" ticks.x [] ticks.y [-4,-2,0,2,4] hidden true
    curve zero #3d3833 expr "0" x_from 0 x_to 4 hidden true
    curve montop #2f6b8f expr mon x_from 0.55 x_to 1.45 hidden true
    region monbar x_from 0.55 x_to 1.45 between ["zero","montop"] style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.35 hidden true
    curve tuetop #b5482e expr tue x_from 2.35 x_to 3.25 hidden true
    region tuebar x_from 2.35 x_to 3.25 between ["zero","tuetop"] style.fill #b5482e style.opacity 0.35 hidden true
    text lab_mon "Monday plans" #2f6b8f at.data [1,-5.3] font_size 26 hidden true
    text lab_tue "Tuesday decides" #b5482e at.data [2.8,-5.3] font_size 26 hidden true
    text val_mon "{mon:1}" #2f6b8f at.data [1,3.4] font_size 28 bind {"at.data.1":"mon + 0.6"} hidden true
    text val_tue "{tue:1}" #b5482e at.data [2.8,0.4] font_size 28 bind {"at.data.1":"max(tue,0) + 0.6"} hidden true
    text pen "Penalty if she skips: +3" #5a8a4a x 830 y 560 font_size 28 hidden true
    text e1 "28% took a locked account" #3d3833 cites ["aky"] x 820 y 560 font_size 28 hidden true
    text e2 "Gym: overconfident" #3d3833 cites ["dvm"] x 820 y 490 font_size 28 hidden true
    text e3 "Effort: present bias" #2f6b8f cites ["ans"] x 820 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true
    text e4 "Money: very little" #b5482e cites ["ans"] x 820 y 490 font_size 30 hidden true
    text c1 "Effort: mostly agreed" #2f6b8f x 800 y 560 font_size 32 hidden true
    text c2 "Size, money: contested" #b5482e x 800 y 480 font_size 32 hidden true
    text c3 "Which self counts: open" #8a5fa8 x 800 y 400 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Ropes, doubts and a test"

So Monday's Ida loses every Tuesday. Unless Monday sees it coming.
    draw ax zero montop monbar val_mon lab_mon tuetop tuebar val_tue lab_tue parallel true

B: If she knows her beta, she can tie her own hands: a bet  (@draw pen@)with a friend, a penalty she (@animate f 3 duration 2@)'d hate to pay if she skips.

Like Ulys (@highlight tuebar effect box duration 2.5@)ses tied to the mast. People do buy such ropes: in the Philippines, about 28 percent  (@draw e1@)of bank clients offered a savings account locked until their goal opened one.
    erase pen

B: The gym members didn't. They bought the flat fee sure their future selves would go. The authors'  (@draw e2@)own reading is overconfidence: present bi (@animate f 0 duration 1.5@)as you don't see coming.

B: But careful with the dollar example. When Ned Augenblick, Muriel Niederle and Charles Sprenger tested it, choices over (@draw e4@) money showed very little present bias.
    erase e1 e2

That makes sense: money today isn't fun today, it can sit in the bank. Effort you feel when you do it.
    highlight e4

B: And over effort t (@draw e3@)hey found considerably more. Work gets pushed later; money, much less.

    erase e3 e4

So present bias over effort and tr (@draw c1@)eats: mostly agreed. How strong it is, and wh (@draw c2@)ether it reaches money: contested.

B: And which Ida is right, Monday's or Tuesday's? Whose wishes count  (@draw c3@)is still argued. Open.

    erase c1 c2 c3

    hide ax zero montop monbar val_mon lab_mon tuetop tuebar val_tue lab_tue

    quiz "On Monday a student plans to start her essay on Wednesday. On Wednesday she plans Friday. What fits best?" reveal true voice b
        intro: Three new cases. First, an essay.
        right: Present bias she doesn't see coming: each day the start sits just past the cliff, so she keeps moving it.
        wrong: Steady impatience never changes its mind, and her plan keeps moving: present bias she doesn't see coming.
        + Present bias she doesn't see coming
        * Steady impatience
        * She simply prefers essays later

    quiz "β = 0.8. A task costs 10 on the day and pays 12 the next day. Planned a month ahead, and then the day comes. What happens?" reveal true
        right: Plans yes, does no: a month out both are shrunk alike, but on the day 12 × 0.8 = 9.6 loses to 10.
        wrong: On the day, compare 10 with 12 × 0.8 = 9.6: plans yes, does no.
        * Plans yes, does yes
        + Plans yes, does no
        * Plans no, does no

    quiz "Who would choose a savings account that locks the money until a date she picks?" reveal true voice b
        right: Someone who knows her bias: the lock stops her future self, and only she sees why she'd want that.
        wrong: A lock only helps if you expect to want the money early: someone who knows her bias.
        + Someone with present bias who knows it
        * Someone with present bias who doesn't know it
        * Someone with no present bias

    show ax zero montop monbar val_mon lab_mon tuetop tuebar val_tue lab_tue

So why do we put off what we planned? The planner sees the effort from a distance; the doer feels it now. Each is reasonable where it stands, on opposite sides of the same cliff.
    highlight monbar tuebar effect box

